## PackageGuard Backend Layout

- **API root**: [`packageguard-api/src/app.js`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/app.js)
- **Config**:
  - Database: [`packageguard-api/src/config/database.js`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/config/database.js)
  - Redis: [`packageguard-api/src/config/redis.js`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/config/redis.js)
  - Storage paths: [`packageguard-api/src/config/storage.js`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/config/storage.js)
- **HTTP layers**:
  - Routes: [`packageguard-api/src/routes`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/routes)
  - Controllers: [`packageguard-api/src/controllers`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/controllers)
  - Services: [`packageguard-api/src/services`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/services)
  - Middleware: [`packageguard-api/src/middleware`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/middleware)
- **Workers**:
  - Evidence processing & signing: [`packageguard-api/src/workers/processClaimWorker.js`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/workers/processClaimWorker.js)
- **Database schema & migrations**:
  - SQL: [`packageguard-api/src/migrations`](mdc:packageguard-api/src/migrations)

The backend follows a **thick server** approach: all business logic (claims, signing, config, validation) should live under `services/` and `workers/`, with controllers staying thin.

